  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/baece8024a1c90a9d83efc2669ecbdda20867f0cebc31670b80deadd36526cf2.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/1943ab4d5fa1cc8358352e325e1dd44d48c45922dabf09813a8568266e466cf7.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134473/assets/01c22233e2876de03185de67738b46116f70e9084d57ea6c0884fc483e920c81.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'",
